WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.860
There is water inside a cell and water all around it.

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.860
So how does water know which way to go?

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.280
To find out, start with something you can see: a drop of dye.

4
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.073
Drop dye into still water.

5
00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:21.113
Each dye molecule jiggles and bumps at random.

6
00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:25.813
Now draw a line down the middle, and count the crossings each way.

7
00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:31.673
At first, far more cross from the crowded side, simply because more are there to

8
00:00:31.673 --> 00:00:34.613
cross. Later the dye is even.

9
00:00:35.973 --> 00:00:38.233
Crossings still happen, equally both ways.

10
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:41.353
The molecules never stopped moving.

11
00:00:42.593 --> 00:00:44.013
There is just no net movement.

12
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:50.223
A difference in concentration from one place to another is a concentration gradient.

13
00:00:51.523 --> 00:00:57.023
In open water, with no energy spent, a substance moves on balance down its gradient,

14
00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:59.063
from more concentrated to less.

15
00:01:00.363 --> 00:01:01.723
That spreading is diffusion.

16
00:01:02.883 --> 00:01:04.883
Now put a cell membrane in the way.

17
00:01:06.203 --> 00:01:11.223
It is two rows of phospholipids, heads facing the water, oily tails tucked inside.

18
00:01:12.623 --> 00:01:15.823
Small oxygen molecules slip straight through the oily middle.

19
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:21.417
Charged ions, such as sodium and chloride bounce off the oily middle.

20
00:01:22.657 --> 00:01:24.237
They cross mainly through proteins.

21
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:29.017
Water crosses too, much of it through protein water channels.

22
00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:33.017
A membrane like this is selectively permeable.

23
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.260
Now join two tanks with this membrane.

24
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.820
Both hold pure water, at the same level.

25
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.060
Water crosses both ways, equally.

26
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.460
Then stir salt into the left tank only.

27
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.820
The salt cannot cross.

28
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:53.333
Water can cross. Salt cannot.

29
00:01:54.693 --> 00:01:58.533
Which tank's water level will rise, the salty one or the pure one?

30
00:01:59.793 --> 00:02:00.953
Pause the film and decide.

31
00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.010
The left tank rises, the salty side.

32
00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.350
Look closely at one drop from each side.

33
00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.990
On the left, some particles in each drop are salt, not water.

34
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.370
So each drop holds fewer water molecules.

35
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.080
So more water molecules reach the membrane from the right each second, and more

36
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.080
cross to the left.

37
00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.240
Water is running down its own gradient.

38
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.760
That net movement of water is osmosis.

39
00:02:35.883 --> 00:02:37.783
Does the left tank rise forever?

40
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:43.743
No. The taller column of water presses back on the membrane.

41
00:02:45.003 --> 00:02:48.223
The rise stops when that push balances the difference in water.

42
00:02:49.317 --> 00:02:51.537
Nothing in this film spent any energy.

43
00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:55.937
Dye, oxygen and water all ran downhill on their own.

44
00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.857
The salt never crossed at all.

45
00:03:00.137 --> 00:03:04.497
Moving anything uphill would cost energy, and that is the second film's story.

46
00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:06.977
So here is the rule.

47
00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:12.357
With no energy spent, whatever can cross runs down its own gradient.

48
00:03:13.697 --> 00:03:16.957
And water runs toward the dissolved particles that cannot cross.
